-- restricted-cash-abandonment-fund-and-debt-reserve
UPDATE public.essays
   SET content = $c$<p><strong>In brief</strong></p><ul><li><p>A bank balance leaves cash and cash equivalents when the entity cannot draw it on demand. An Indonesian contractor's abandonment fund sits in a joint account with SKK Migas, and a debt service reserve sits in an account the lenders control, so both are restricted cash.</p></li><li><p>The current/non-current split follows when the restriction lifts or the money is spent. The same deposit can change sides without moving, and no such reclassification is a cash flow.</p></li><li><p>The abandonment fund and the abandonment provision are presented gross. No standard lets one cancel the other, and under a cost-recovery contract any surplus left in the fund at the end belongs to the state, which caps what the fund is worth to the contractor.</p></li></ul><hr><h2>Restricted cash grew 11 percent while cash and cash equivalents nearly halved, and the face of the balance sheet cannot say what it secures</h2><p>The balance sheet on this page reports restricted cash, non-current, of 1,890,493 at 31 December 2024 against 1,700,675 a year earlier, in thousands, a rise of 189,818 or 11.2 percent. The current line rose 15.5 percent to 154,217, while cash and cash equivalents fell 47.9 percent to 2,614,318. The non-current line is now 6.24 percent of total assets (1,890,493 / 30,303,898) and 72.3 percent of cash and cash equivalents, against 33.9 percent a year earlier.</p><p>This essay uses PT Hulu Energi Nusantara ("HEN"), an upstream contractor operating production sharing contracts (PSCs) with SKK Migas, the regulator of Indonesian upstream contracts. HEN reports in USD under Indonesian SAK with a 31 December year end. HEN and every figure attached to it are constructed for this essay alone, and HEN amounts are in USD thousand.</p><p>HEN holds two restricted balances. Its Kenari field is operated under a cost-recovery PSC, one in which the contractor recovers its approved costs from production before the remainder is split with the state, and HEN holds 100 percent. Kenari has an abandonment and site restoration (ASR) fund, money set aside to plug wells and remove facilities when production ends, and an ASR provision, the liability for that future work. HEN's reserve-based lending (RBL) facility, a bank loan sized on the value of its reserves, requires a debt service reserve account (DSRA) holding 4,500. The CFO's draft 2025 statements make three choices. Cash and cash equivalents include the DSRA's 4,500, because the account is in HEN's name and earns interest. The ASR provision is shown net of the fund at 17,305, since "the fund exists only to pay it". The cash flow statement shows the 3,000 paid from the fund for plugging four wells as an operating outflow, with a matching investing inflow. The auditor objects to all three.</p><p>The governing claim of this essay is that restricted cash is defined by who can withdraw it and when, so its classification, cash flow presentation and link to the ASR provision follow from the terms of the account.</p><p>This is a mechanism essay. Its verdict is that HEN's cash and cash equivalents fall by 4,500, that its restricted cash is 30,500, that the ASR provision of 43,305 stands gross beside the 26,000 fund, and that the 3,000 plugging payment never touched cash and cash equivalents. The standards are PSAK 207 <em>Statement of Cash Flows</em> (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7), PSAK 201 <em>Presentation of Financial Statements</em> (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) and PSAK 237 <em>Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets</em> (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37).</p><h2>A balance stops being cash when the entity cannot draw it on demand, and a restriction on its use is not enough</h2><p>Cash is money the entity can withdraw today. PSAK 207 defines it as cash on hand and demand deposits, and cash equivalents as short-term, highly liquid investments held to meet short-term cash commitments and readily convertible to known amounts of cash. "Restricted cash" has no definition in the standards; preparers use the label for balances kept apart because of limits on their use.</p><p>The test is narrower than "earmarked". In 2022 the IFRS Interpretations Committee considered a demand deposit that a seller had agreed with a buyer to use only for warranty claims but could still withdraw at any time, and concluded it remained cash. A contractual restriction on use changes the classification only if the deposit is no longer withdrawable on demand. The restriction is still disclosed, since PSAK 207 requires disclosure of significant balances not available for use by the group.</p><p>HEN's two balances fail the test for different reasons.</p><ul><li><p>The ASR fund fails because of who must sign. Under Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15 of 2018 on post-operation activities, contractors reserve ASR funds in stages under the approved work program and budget (Art. 11(2)), starting in the year production is declared commercial, in a joint account between SKK Migas and the contractor at a state-owned commercial bank (Art. 11(3)). HEN cannot withdraw them alone, so they are not demand deposits for HEN.</p></li><li><p>The DSRA fails because the bank will not pay out. The RBL's account control agreement, an agreement among HEN, the lenders and the account bank that tells the bank whose instructions to follow, requires the bank to refuse HEN's withdrawals while the loan is outstanding unless the lenders consent.</p></li></ul><p>Removing the DSRA lowers cash and cash equivalents by 4,500. Restricted cash at 31 December 2025 is 26,000 + 4,500 = 30,500.</p><h2>The twelve-month line follows when the restriction lifts or the money is spent, so a deposit can change sides without moving</h2><p>A restricted balance is current when, within twelve months, the restriction lifts or the money is applied to the obligation it secures. That follows from PSAK 201, which classifies cash as current unless it is restricted from being exchanged or used to settle a liability for at least twelve months after the reporting date.</p><p>Both events occur at HEN.</p><ul><li><p>In November 2024 SKK Migas approved plugging four idle Kenari wells in 2025 at 3,000, paid from the fund, so at 31 December 2024 that 3,000 is current.</p></li><li><p>In November 2025 SKK Migas approved 2,500 of plugging for three more wells in 2026. At 31 December 2025, 2,500 of the fund is current.</p></li><li><p>The DSRA is released when the RBL is repaid on 30 June 2026, eighteen months after 31 December 2024 (non-current) and six months after 31 December 2025 (current).</p></li></ul><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 December, USD thousand</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>ASR fund, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>3,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>DSRA, current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Restricted cash, current</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>0</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>3,000</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>7,000</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>ASR fund, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,500</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>DSRA, non-current</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,500</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Restricted cash, non-current</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>27,500</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>27,500</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>23,500</strong></p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>Total restricted cash</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>27,500</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>30,500</strong></p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p><strong>30,500</strong></p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Total restricted cash is 30,500 at the 2024 and 2025 year ends, while 4,000 moves from non-current to current. The DSRA's 4,500 crossed the line with no transaction at all. The rest of the fund stays non-current until the field stops producing, which HEN expects in 2034.</p><h2>A movement in restricted cash is a cash flow only when it crosses the cash boundary, and its activity class is a judgment</h2><p>The cash flow statement explains the change in cash and cash equivalents, so a deposit from HEN's operating account into the fund is an outflow, while interest credited inside the fund, a payment from the fund to a vendor and a reclassification are not.</p><p>Interest at a constructed 3 percent of the opening balance is credited in early December, before the deposit and any drawing.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Kenari ASR fund, USD thousand</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Opening balance</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>20,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>26,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Interest at 3% of opening</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>690</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>780</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Deposit from HEN's operating account</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,400</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,310</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2,220</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Paid to plugging vendor</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(3,000)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Closing balance</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>23,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>26,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>26,000</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>HEN booked three entries in 2025.</p><blockquote><p>Dr Restricted cash, ASR fund 2,220 / Cr Cash and cash equivalents 2,220 (deposit)</p><p>Dr Restricted cash, ASR fund 780 / Cr Finance income 780 (interest credited to the fund)</p><p>Dr ASR provision 3,000 / Cr Restricted cash, ASR fund 3,000 (fund pays the plugging vendor)</p></blockquote><p>The classification of the 2,220 is a policy choice. PSAK 207 allows a payment in investing activities only if it results in a recognised asset, and the deposit creates one, so HEN classifies deposits as investing. Operating can be argued, because the PSC treats the deposit as an operating cost. HEN's earlier statements used investing, and PSAK 208 requires a policy to be applied consistently. HEN presented the DSRA's funding (4,500 in 2023) as financing, because the account exists only because of the loan, and will present its release on repayment in June 2026 the same way; investing would also be defensible.</p><table style="min-width: 100px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Cash flow statement, USD thousand</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2024</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>2025</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Investing: deposits into ASR fund</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,400)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,310)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(2,220)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Financing: funding of DSRA</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(4,500)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>HEN uses the indirect method, which starts from profit and removes non-cash items, so the 780 of interest is deducted and the 2,205 of unwinding on the provision (next section) added back. The 3,000 plugging payment settled the provision from a restricted asset, so it stays out of the statement and is disclosed as a non-cash transaction. The draft's pair of 3,000 flows would be right only if the fund had reimbursed HEN's operating account, which then paid the vendor.</p><h2>The fund and the provision stay gross, because no route in the standards lets one cancel the other</h2><p>The Kenari provision grows each year by unwinding even when no new obligation arises. Under PSAK 237, as in the owner's study notes, the present value of the expected removal and restoration cost is added to the asset and recognised as a liability, and the discount unwinds each year as a finance cost. The Kenari provision stood at 40,000 on 1 January 2023, discounted at a constructed 5 percent.</p><blockquote><p>2023: 40,000 + 40,000 × 5% = 42,000</p><p>2024: 42,000 + 42,000 × 5% = 44,100</p><p>2025: 44,100 + 44,100 × 5% − 3,000 used for plugging = 43,305</p></blockquote><blockquote><p>Dr Finance cost, unwinding of discount 2,205 / Cr ASR provision 2,205</p></blockquote><p>Like the fund, the provision at 31 December 2025 splits into 2,500 current and 40,805 non-current. The draft nets the two to 17,305. Three routes could permit that, and each fails.</p><ol><li><p>PSAK 201 prohibits offsetting assets and liabilities unless a standard requires or permits it.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 232 (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) permits offsetting a financial asset and a financial liability only with a legally enforceable right of set-off and an intention to settle net or simultaneously. The provision is an obligation to perform work, not a financial liability, and HEN has no right of set-off.</p></li><li><p>When another party will reimburse expenditure on a provision, PSAK 237 recognises the reimbursement, once virtually certain, as a separate asset no larger than the provision; only the expense may be shown net. The fund holds HEN's own deposits, so the rule may not apply, and if it does, it keeps the fund on the asset side.</p></li></ol><p>IFRIC 5 covers decommissioning funds whose assets are administered separately and to which contributors' access is restricted. A contributor without control, joint control or significant influence over the fund (the power to direct it, alone or with others, or to take part in its policy decisions) keeps its obligation and its interest separate, measuring the interest at the lower of the obligation and its share of the fair value of the fund's net assets. The Indonesian Institute of Accountants (IAI) has not adopted IFRIC 5, listing it in a June 2022 public hearing as not relevant to Indonesian fact patterns. A contractor could consult it only through the hierarchy of PSAK 208 (formerly 25, IAS 8), which allows IFRS pronouncements to be considered where no SAK addresses an issue. Every route leaves the two items gross.</p><p>The IFRIC 5 cap matters more than its scope. Under Art. 15 of Regulation 15/2018, any difference between actual post-operation cost and the fund reserved reduces or increases operating cost, with SKK Migas approval. Under Art. 16, whatever remains when a cost-recovery contract ends belongs to the state; under a gross split contract, a PSC with no cost recovery in which gross production is split and the contractor bears its own costs, it returns to the contractor. A cost-recovery contractor therefore benefits from the fund only up to the cost it must incur. Kenari's fund of 26,000 is below the provision of 43,305, so the cap does not bind in 2025. If a revision cut the provision below the fund, the essay's reading is that the excess should not be carried as HEN's asset, since HEN can never use it.</p><h2>Interest belongs to the fund, and under cost recovery the state shares its value</h2><p>Regulation 15/2018 counts interest earned on the fund toward the amount the contractor must reserve (Art. 11(4)). HEN's approved work program requires the Kenari fund to receive 3,000 a year from deposits and interest combined, so the deposit is the remainder.</p><blockquote><p>2023 deposit = 3,000 − 600 = 2,400</p><p>2024 deposit = 3,000 − 690 = 2,310</p><p>2025 deposit = 3,000 − 780 = 2,220</p></blockquote><p>HEN records the 780 as finance income, because the fund is its asset. Under a cost-recovery PSC the deposit is a recoverable operating cost (Art. 12(1)), so each dollar of interest cuts HEN's cost recovery by a dollar and leaves more production to split, of which the state takes its contractual share; this essay does not model the split. Under a gross split contract the deposit is a deductible cost the contractor bears (Art. 12(2)) and the surplus returns to it, so the interest benefits the contractor directly.</p><h2>Deposits are deducted when made and expensed with the provision, and the difference is deferred tax</h2><p>PSAK 212 (formerly 46, IAS 12) measures deferred tax on temporary differences, the gaps between an item's carrying amount and its tax base (the amount the tax computation will recognise for it), which reverse in later years. This section assumes, as the essay's reading of the PSC terms, that each deposit is recovered and deducted in the year it is made, that fund interest is neither taxed nor deducted, and that plugging paid from the fund gives no second deduction. Those assumptions fix each item's tax base.</p><ul><li><p>the fund settles costs that are neither taxable nor deductible when paid, so its tax base equals its carrying amount;</p></li><li><p>the ASR cost in oil and gas properties is never deducted itself, so its tax base is nil (carrying amount 24,000 on 1 January 2023, less units-of-production depreciation of a constructed 2,000 a year);</p></li><li><p>the provision's future deductions are the deposits still to come, which in today's money equal the provision less the fund, so its tax base is the fund.</p></li></ul><table style="min-width: 75px;"><colgroup><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"><col style="min-width: 25px;"></colgroup><tbody><tr><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>USD thousand</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1 Jan 2023</p></th><th colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>31 Dec 2025</p></th></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>ASR asset, taxable difference (carrying amount; tax base nil)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>24,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>18,000</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>ASR provision, deductible difference (provision − fund)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(20,000)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>(17,305)</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Fund (tax base = carrying amount)</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>0</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Net taxable temporary difference</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>4,000</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>695</p></td></tr><tr><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>Deferred tax liability at 40%</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>1,600</p></td><td colspan="1" rowspan="1"><p>278</p></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The net difference fell by 4,000 − 695 = 3,305, so 2023–2025 carry a deferred tax credit of 3,305 × 40% = 1,322, at HEN's combined PSC rate of 25% + 20% × (1 − 25%) = 40%. The income statement gives the same 3,305: net book charges of 10,235 (unwinding 6,305 + depreciation 6,000 − fund interest 2,070) less deductions of 6,930 (2,400 + 2,310 + 2,220). The differences reverse because Art. 15 adds or deducts any shortfall or surplus as operating cost, so total deductions come to the ASR cost less interest, the same as total net book charges. Allocating the differences among the three items is a judgment preparers resolve differently. Since a 2021 amendment, adopted in PSAK 46 from 2023, the initial recognition exemption no longer lets entities skip deferred tax on decommissioning assets and liabilities recognised together.</p><h2>Corrected, HEN moves 4,500 out of cash, shows 30,500 of restricted cash and keeps the 43,305 provision gross</h2><p>HEN's corrected 31 December 2025 statements reverse all three draft choices.</p><blockquote><p>Cash and cash equivalents = draft figure − 4,500 DSRA</p><p>Restricted cash, current = 2,500 ASR + 4,500 DSRA = 7,000</p><p>Restricted cash, non-current = 26,000 − 2,500 = 23,500</p><p>ASR provision = 43,305 (current 2,500; non-current 40,805), gross of the fund</p><p>Cash flow statement, 2025: deposit of 2,220 in investing; plugging of 3,000 disclosed as non-cash; interest of 780 deducted and unwinding of 2,205 added back in the operating reconciliation</p></blockquote><p>The draft's 17,305 is the unfunded gap measured in today's money. Because the provision accretes at 5 percent and the fund earns 3 percent, the deposits still to come are larger in nominal terms. The ASR provision is 2.5 times the netted figure (43,305 / 17,305).</p><p>The reported line on this page may hold abandonment funds, debt reserves, cash collateral for bank guarantees, or all three. To read it, the notes must say what the money secures, when each part comes free, which activity class the transfers use, and how the ASR fund compares with the ASR provision.</p><h3>Least-confident assertions</h3><ul><li><p>The PSC mechanics were confirmed against the full text of Regulation 15/2018 (Arts. 11, 12, 15, 16). Art. 20 lets SKK Migas set the amount and method for older contracts without an ASR clause. Whether the regulation has since been amended was not confirmed.</p></li><li><p>Art. 17 passes the obligation and the fund to a successor contractor if the PSC ends and a new contractor takes over. The essay assumes HEN operates Kenari to the end of production; a handover would limit how much of the fund HEN ever uses.</p></li><li><p>The timing of recovery and deduction, and the tax treatment of fund interest, are assumed, and the tax base of the provision rests on them. If deposits were deductible only when spent from the fund, the deferred tax figures would change.</p></li><li><p>Treating the DSRA as outside cash depends on the account control agreement. A DSRA HEN could draw freely, subject only to a covenant, would stay in cash under the 2022 agenda decision.</p></li><li><p>Capping the fund at the provision under cost recovery is the essay's reading, by analogy with IFRIC 5. Indonesian contractors appear to present the fund at its full balance, and the investing and financing classes are HEN's policy choices, not a survey of practice.</p></li></ul><h2>References</h2><ol><li><p>Owner's study notes, "Inter 2 - Chapter 13 - Current Liabilities.pdf" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), section on environmental provisions (capitalisation into the asset, unwinding as interest, settlement entry). NOTES. The notes measure the liability at a market-based fair value; PSAK 237 uses the best estimate, discounted, which this essay follows.</p></li><li><p>Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15 of 2018 on post-operation activities in upstream oil and gas (Berita Negara 2018 No. 305), Arts. 11, 12, 15, 16, 17 and 20. VERIFIED (full text, JDIH ESDM PDF, read by the independent recheck); the ESDM website summary of Arts. 11–16 was also read for v0.1.</p></li><li><p>IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision <em>Demand Deposits with Restrictions on Use arising from a Contract with a Third Party (IAS 7)</em>, 2022, as reported by BDO, with IAS 7 paragraphs 6 and 48 and IAS 1 paragraph 66(d). VERIFIED (web, BDO summary).</p></li><li><p>IAI/DSAK, <em>Materi Presentasi Public Hearing DSAK IAI</em>, 21 June 2022, table of IFRS pronouncements not adopted: IFRIC 5, "tidak relevan dengan pola fakta di Indonesia". VERIFIED (web, IAI PDF opened).</p></li><li><p>IAI, <em>Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia</em> (renumbering table): PSAK 2 → 207, PSAK 1 → 201, PSAK 57 → 237, PSAK 50 → 232, PSAK 25 → 208, PSAK 46 → 212; no interpretation listed for IFRIC 5. VERIFIED (web, IAI PDF; title column misaligned in extraction, number pairs read).</p></li><li><p>IFRIC 5 <em>Rights to Interests arising from Decommissioning, Restoration and Environmental Rehabilitation Funds</em>, scope and paragraphs 8–9 (lower of the obligation and the share of the fair value of the fund's net assets). VERIFIED (web excerpts via XRB NZ IFRIC 5, BDO and IAS Plus, read by the independent recheck; full text not opened by the author).</p></li><li><p>Pertamina Hulu Energi, 2023 annual report, note on restricted funds for site restoration presented within other non-current assets. NOT VERIFIED (seen only in a search-result summary).</p></li><li><p>PSAK 207 <em>Laporan Arus Kas</em> (IAS 7), beyond paragraphs 6 and 48: investing classification, non-cash transactions. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 201 <em>Penyajian Laporan Keuangan</em> (IAS 1), offsetting prohibition. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 232 <em>Instrumen Keuangan: Penyajian</em> (IAS 32), offsetting. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 237 <em>Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi</em> (IAS 37), reimbursements. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 212 <em>Pajak Penghasilan</em> (IAS 12), including the 2021 single-transaction amendment (adopted as an amendment to PSAK 46, effective 1 January 2023, per the independent recheck). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li><li><p>PSAK 208 <em>Kebijakan Akuntansi, Perubahan Estimasi Akuntansi, dan Kesalahan</em> (IAS 8), hierarchy and consistency. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.</p></li></ol>$c$,
       content_json = $j${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In brief","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A bank balance leaves cash and cash equivalents when the entity cannot draw it on demand. An Indonesian contractor's abandonment fund sits in a joint account with SKK Migas, and a debt service reserve sits in an account the lenders control, so both are restricted cash.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The current/non-current split follows when the restriction lifts or the money is spent. The same deposit can change sides without moving, and no such reclassification is a cash flow.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The abandonment fund and the abandonment provision are presented gross. No standard lets one cancel the other, and under a cost-recovery contract any surplus left in the fund at the end belongs to the state, which caps what the fund is worth to the contractor.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"horizontalRule"},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restricted cash grew 11 percent while cash and cash equivalents nearly halved, and the face of the balance sheet cannot say what it secures","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The balance sheet on this page reports restricted cash, non-current, of 1,890,493 at 31 December 2024 against 1,700,675 a year earlier, in thousands, a rise of 189,818 or 11.2 percent. The current line rose 15.5 percent to 154,217, while cash and cash equivalents fell 47.9 percent to 2,614,318. The non-current line is now 6.24 percent of total assets (1,890,493 / 30,303,898) and 72.3 percent of cash and cash equivalents, against 33.9 percent a year earlier.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"This essay uses PT Hulu Energi Nusantara (\"HEN\"), an upstream contractor operating production sharing contracts (PSCs) with SKK Migas, the regulator of Indonesian upstream contracts. HEN reports in USD under Indonesian SAK with a 31 December year end. HEN and every figure attached to it are constructed for this essay alone, and HEN amounts are in USD thousand.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN holds two restricted balances. Its Kenari field is operated under a cost-recovery PSC, one in which the contractor recovers its approved costs from production before the remainder is split with the state, and HEN holds 100 percent. Kenari has an abandonment and site restoration (ASR) fund, money set aside to plug wells and remove facilities when production ends, and an ASR provision, the liability for that future work. HEN's reserve-based lending (RBL) facility, a bank loan sized on the value of its reserves, requires a debt service reserve account (DSRA) holding 4,500. The CFO's draft 2025 statements make three choices. Cash and cash equivalents include the DSRA's 4,500, because the account is in HEN's name and earns interest. The ASR provision is shown net of the fund at 17,305, since \"the fund exists only to pay it\". The cash flow statement shows the 3,000 paid from the fund for plugging four wells as an operating outflow, with a matching investing inflow. The auditor objects to all three.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The governing claim of this essay is that restricted cash is defined by who can withdraw it and when, so its classification, cash flow presentation and link to the ASR provision follow from the terms of the account.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"This is a mechanism essay. Its verdict is that HEN's cash and cash equivalents fall by 4,500, that its restricted cash is 30,500, that the ASR provision of 43,305 stands gross beside the 26,000 fund, and that the 3,000 plugging payment never touched cash and cash equivalents. The standards are PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Statement of Cash Flows","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 2, IAS 7), PSAK 201 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Presentation of Financial Statements","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 1, IAS 1) and PSAK 237 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisions, Contingent Liabilities and Contingent Assets","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (formerly PSAK 57, IAS 37).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A balance stops being cash when the entity cannot draw it on demand, and a restriction on its use is not enough","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash is money the entity can withdraw today. PSAK 207 defines it as cash on hand and demand deposits, and cash equivalents as short-term, highly liquid investments held to meet short-term cash commitments and readily convertible to known amounts of cash. \"Restricted cash\" has no definition in the standards; preparers use the label for balances kept apart because of limits on their use.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The test is narrower than \"earmarked\". In 2022 the IFRS Interpretations Committee considered a demand deposit that a seller had agreed with a buyer to use only for warranty claims but could still withdraw at any time, and concluded it remained cash. A contractual restriction on use changes the classification only if the deposit is no longer withdrawable on demand. The restriction is still disclosed, since PSAK 207 requires disclosure of significant balances not available for use by the group.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN's two balances fail the test for different reasons.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The ASR fund fails because of who must sign. Under Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15 of 2018 on post-operation activities, contractors reserve ASR funds in stages under the approved work program and budget (Art. 11(2)), starting in the year production is declared commercial, in a joint account between SKK Migas and the contractor at a state-owned commercial bank (Art. 11(3)). HEN cannot withdraw them alone, so they are not demand deposits for HEN.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The DSRA fails because the bank will not pay out. The RBL's account control agreement, an agreement among HEN, the lenders and the account bank that tells the bank whose instructions to follow, requires the bank to refuse HEN's withdrawals while the loan is outstanding unless the lenders consent.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Removing the DSRA lowers cash and cash equivalents by 4,500. Restricted cash at 31 December 2025 is 26,000 + 4,500 = 30,500.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The twelve-month line follows when the restriction lifts or the money is spent, so a deposit can change sides without moving","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A restricted balance is current when, within twelve months, the restriction lifts or the money is applied to the obligation it secures. That follows from PSAK 201, which classifies cash as current unless it is restricted from being exchanged or used to settle a liability for at least twelve months after the reporting date.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Both events occur at HEN.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In November 2024 SKK Migas approved plugging four idle Kenari wells in 2025 at 3,000, paid from the fund, so at 31 December 2024 that 3,000 is current.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"In November 2025 SKK Migas approved 2,500 of plugging for three more wells in 2026. At 31 December 2025, 2,500 of the fund is current.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The DSRA is released when the RBL is repaid on 30 June 2026, eighteen months after 31 December 2024 (non-current) and six months after 31 December 2025 (current).","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 December, USD thousand","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ASR fund, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DSRA, current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restricted cash, current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"3,000","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"7,000","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ASR fund, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,500","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"DSRA, non-current","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,500","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restricted cash, non-current","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"27,500","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"27,500","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,500","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total restricted cash","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"27,500","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,500","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"30,500","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Total restricted cash is 30,500 at the 2024 and 2025 year ends, while 4,000 moves from non-current to current. The DSRA's 4,500 crossed the line with no transaction at all. The rest of the fund stays non-current until the field stops producing, which HEN expects in 2034.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"A movement in restricted cash is a cash flow only when it crosses the cash boundary, and its activity class is a judgment","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The cash flow statement explains the change in cash and cash equivalents, so a deposit from HEN's operating account into the fund is an outflow, while interest credited inside the fund, a payment from the fund to a vendor and a reclassification are not.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest at a constructed 3 percent of the opening balance is credited in early December, before the deposit and any drawing.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Kenari ASR fund, USD thousand","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Opening balance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"20,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"26,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest at 3% of opening","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"690","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"780","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deposit from HEN's operating account","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,400","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,310","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2,220","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Paid to plugging vendor","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(3,000)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Closing balance","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"23,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"26,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"26,000","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN booked three entries in 2025.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Restricted cash, ASR fund 2,220 / Cr Cash and cash equivalents 2,220 (deposit)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Restricted cash, ASR fund 780 / Cr Finance income 780 (interest credited to the fund)","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr ASR provision 3,000 / Cr Restricted cash, ASR fund 3,000 (fund pays the plugging vendor)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The classification of the 2,220 is a policy choice. PSAK 207 allows a payment in investing activities only if it results in a recognised asset, and the deposit creates one, so HEN classifies deposits as investing. Operating can be argued, because the PSC treats the deposit as an operating cost. HEN's earlier statements used investing, and PSAK 208 requires a policy to be applied consistently. HEN presented the DSRA's funding (4,500 in 2023) as financing, because the account exists only because of the loan, and will present its release on repayment in June 2026 the same way; investing would also be defensible.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow statement, USD thousand","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Investing: deposits into ASR fund","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2,400)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2,310)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(2,220)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Financing: funding of DSRA","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(4,500)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN uses the indirect method, which starts from profit and removes non-cash items, so the 780 of interest is deducted and the 2,205 of unwinding on the provision (next section) added back. The 3,000 plugging payment settled the provision from a restricted asset, so it stays out of the statement and is disclosed as a non-cash transaction. The draft's pair of 3,000 flows would be right only if the fund had reimbursed HEN's operating account, which then paid the vendor.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The fund and the provision stay gross, because no route in the standards lets one cancel the other","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The Kenari provision grows each year by unwinding even when no new obligation arises. Under PSAK 237, as in the owner's study notes, the present value of the expected removal and restoration cost is added to the asset and recognised as a liability, and the discount unwinds each year as a finance cost. The Kenari provision stood at 40,000 on 1 January 2023, discounted at a constructed 5 percent.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023: 40,000 + 40,000 × 5% = 42,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024: 42,000 + 42,000 × 5% = 44,100","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025: 44,100 + 44,100 × 5% − 3,000 used for plugging = 43,305","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Dr Finance cost, unwinding of discount 2,205 / Cr ASR provision 2,205","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Like the fund, the provision at 31 December 2025 splits into 2,500 current and 40,805 non-current. The draft nets the two to 17,305. Three routes could permit that, and each fails.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 prohibits offsetting assets and liabilities unless a standard requires or permits it.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 232 (formerly PSAK 50, IAS 32) permits offsetting a financial asset and a financial liability only with a legally enforceable right of set-off and an intention to settle net or simultaneously. The provision is an obligation to perform work, not a financial liability, and HEN has no right of set-off.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"When another party will reimburse expenditure on a provision, PSAK 237 recognises the reimbursement, once virtually certain, as a separate asset no larger than the provision; only the expense may be shown net. The fund holds HEN's own deposits, so the rule may not apply, and if it does, it keeps the fund on the asset side.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRIC 5 covers decommissioning funds whose assets are administered separately and to which contributors' access is restricted. A contributor without control, joint control or significant influence over the fund (the power to direct it, alone or with others, or to take part in its policy decisions) keeps its obligation and its interest separate, measuring the interest at the lower of the obligation and its share of the fair value of the fund's net assets. The Indonesian Institute of Accountants (IAI) has not adopted IFRIC 5, listing it in a June 2022 public hearing as not relevant to Indonesian fact patterns. A contractor could consult it only through the hierarchy of PSAK 208 (formerly 25, IAS 8), which allows IFRS pronouncements to be considered where no SAK addresses an issue. Every route leaves the two items gross.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The IFRIC 5 cap matters more than its scope. Under Art. 15 of Regulation 15/2018, any difference between actual post-operation cost and the fund reserved reduces or increases operating cost, with SKK Migas approval. Under Art. 16, whatever remains when a cost-recovery contract ends belongs to the state; under a gross split contract, a PSC with no cost recovery in which gross production is split and the contractor bears its own costs, it returns to the contractor. A cost-recovery contractor therefore benefits from the fund only up to the cost it must incur. Kenari's fund of 26,000 is below the provision of 43,305, so the cap does not bind in 2025. If a revision cut the provision below the fund, the essay's reading is that the excess should not be carried as HEN's asset, since HEN can never use it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Interest belongs to the fund, and under cost recovery the state shares its value","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Regulation 15/2018 counts interest earned on the fund toward the amount the contractor must reserve (Art. 11(4)). HEN's approved work program requires the Kenari fund to receive 3,000 a year from deposits and interest combined, so the deposit is the remainder.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2023 deposit = 3,000 − 600 = 2,400","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2024 deposit = 3,000 − 690 = 2,310","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"2025 deposit = 3,000 − 780 = 2,220","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN records the 780 as finance income, because the fund is its asset. Under a cost-recovery PSC the deposit is a recoverable operating cost (Art. 12(1)), so each dollar of interest cuts HEN's cost recovery by a dollar and leaves more production to split, of which the state takes its contractual share; this essay does not model the split. Under a gross split contract the deposit is a deductible cost the contractor bears (Art. 12(2)) and the surplus returns to it, so the interest benefits the contractor directly.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deposits are deducted when made and expensed with the provision, and the difference is deferred tax","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 212 (formerly 46, IAS 12) measures deferred tax on temporary differences, the gaps between an item's carrying amount and its tax base (the amount the tax computation will recognise for it), which reverse in later years. This section assumes, as the essay's reading of the PSC terms, that each deposit is recovered and deducted in the year it is made, that fund interest is neither taxed nor deducted, and that plugging paid from the fund gives no second deduction. Those assumptions fix each item's tax base.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the fund settles costs that are neither taxable nor deductible when paid, so its tax base equals its carrying amount;","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the ASR cost in oil and gas properties is never deducted itself, so its tax base is nil (carrying amount 24,000 on 1 January 2023, less units-of-production depreciation of a constructed 2,000 a year);","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"the provision's future deductions are the deposits still to come, which in today's money equal the provision less the fund, so its tax base is the fund.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"USD thousand","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1 Jan 2023","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"31 Dec 2025","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ASR asset, taxable difference (carrying amount; tax base nil)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"24,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"18,000","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ASR provision, deductible difference (provision − fund)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(20,000)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"(17,305)","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Fund (tax base = carrying amount)","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"0","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Net taxable temporary difference","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"4,000","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"695","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Deferred tax liability at 40%","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"1,600","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"align":null,"colspan":1,"rowspan":1,"colwidth":null},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"278","type":"text"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The net difference fell by 4,000 − 695 = 3,305, so 2023–2025 carry a deferred tax credit of 3,305 × 40% = 1,322, at HEN's combined PSC rate of 25% + 20% × (1 − 25%) = 40%. The income statement gives the same 3,305: net book charges of 10,235 (unwinding 6,305 + depreciation 6,000 − fund interest 2,070) less deductions of 6,930 (2,400 + 2,310 + 2,220). The differences reverse because Art. 15 adds or deducts any shortfall or surplus as operating cost, so total deductions come to the ASR cost less interest, the same as total net book charges. Allocating the differences among the three items is a judgment preparers resolve differently. Since a 2021 amendment, adopted in PSAK 46 from 2023, the initial recognition exemption no longer lets entities skip deferred tax on decommissioning assets and liabilities recognised together.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Corrected, HEN moves 4,500 out of cash, shows 30,500 of restricted cash and keeps the 43,305 provision gross","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"HEN's corrected 31 December 2025 statements reverse all three draft choices.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"blockquote","attrs":{"variant":"quote"},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash and cash equivalents = draft figure − 4,500 DSRA","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restricted cash, current = 2,500 ASR + 4,500 DSRA = 7,000","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Restricted cash, non-current = 26,000 − 2,500 = 23,500","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"ASR provision = 43,305 (current 2,500; non-current 40,805), gross of the fund","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Cash flow statement, 2025: deposit of 2,220 in investing; plugging of 3,000 disclosed as non-cash; interest of 780 deducted and unwinding of 2,205 added back in the operating reconciliation","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The draft's 17,305 is the unfunded gap measured in today's money. Because the provision accretes at 5 percent and the fund earns 3 percent, the deposits still to come are larger in nominal terms. The ASR provision is 2.5 times the netted figure (43,305 / 17,305).","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The reported line on this page may hold abandonment funds, debt reserves, cash collateral for bank guarantees, or all three. To read it, the notes must say what the money secures, when each part comes free, which activity class the transfers use, and how the ASR fund compares with the ASR provision.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Least-confident assertions","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The PSC mechanics were confirmed against the full text of Regulation 15/2018 (Arts. 11, 12, 15, 16). Art. 20 lets SKK Migas set the amount and method for older contracts without an ASR clause. Whether the regulation has since been amended was not confirmed.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Art. 17 passes the obligation and the fund to a successor contractor if the PSC ends and a new contractor takes over. The essay assumes HEN operates Kenari to the end of production; a handover would limit how much of the fund HEN ever uses.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"The timing of recovery and deduction, and the tax treatment of fund interest, are assumed, and the tax base of the provision rests on them. If deposits were deductible only when spent from the fund, the deferred tax figures would change.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Treating the DSRA as outside cash depends on the account control agreement. A DSRA HEN could draw freely, subject only to a covenant, would stay in cash under the 2022 agenda decision.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Capping the fund at the provision under cost recovery is the essay's reading, by analogy with IFRIC 5. Indonesian contractors appear to present the fund at its full balance, and the investing and financing classes are HEN's policy choices, not a survey of practice.","type":"text"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2,"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"References","type":"text"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"type":null,"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Owner's study notes, \"Inter 2 - Chapter 13 - Current Liabilities.pdf\" (Rumpun Akuntansi Keuangan), section on environmental provisions (capitalisation into the asset, unwinding as interest, settlement entry). NOTES. The notes measure the liability at a market-based fair value; PSAK 237 uses the best estimate, discounted, which this essay follows.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Minister of Energy and Mineral Resources Regulation 15 of 2018 on post-operation activities in upstream oil and gas (Berita Negara 2018 No. 305), Arts. 11, 12, 15, 16, 17 and 20. VERIFIED (full text, JDIH ESDM PDF, read by the independent recheck); the ESDM website summary of Arts. 11–16 was also read for v0.1.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRS Interpretations Committee, agenda decision ","type":"text"},{"text":"Demand Deposits with Restrictions on Use arising from a Contract with a Third Party (IAS 7)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", 2022, as reported by BDO, with IAS 7 paragraphs 6 and 48 and IAS 1 paragraph 66(d). VERIFIED (web, BDO summary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IAI/DSAK, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Materi Presentasi Public Hearing DSAK IAI","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", 21 June 2022, table of IFRS pronouncements not adopted: IFRIC 5, \"tidak relevan dengan pola fakta di Indonesia\". VERIFIED (web, IAI PDF opened).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IAI, ","type":"text"},{"text":"Perubahan Penomoran PSAK dan ISAK dalam SAK Indonesia","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (renumbering table): PSAK 2 → 207, PSAK 1 → 201, PSAK 57 → 237, PSAK 50 → 232, PSAK 25 → 208, PSAK 46 → 212; no interpretation listed for IFRIC 5. VERIFIED (web, IAI PDF; title column misaligned in extraction, number pairs read).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"IFRIC 5 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Rights to Interests arising from Decommissioning, Restoration and Environmental Rehabilitation Funds","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", scope and paragraphs 8–9 (lower of the obligation and the share of the fair value of the fund's net assets). VERIFIED (web excerpts via XRB NZ IFRIC 5, BDO and IAS Plus, read by the independent recheck; full text not opened by the author).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"Pertamina Hulu Energi, 2023 annual report, note on restricted funds for site restoration presented within other non-current assets. NOT VERIFIED (seen only in a search-result summary).","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 207 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Laporan Arus Kas","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 7), beyond paragraphs 6 and 48: investing classification, non-cash transactions. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 201 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Penyajian Laporan Keuangan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 1), offsetting prohibition. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 232 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Instrumen Keuangan: Penyajian","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 32), offsetting. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 237 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Provisi, Liabilitas Kontinjensi, dan Aset Kontinjensi","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 37), reimbursements. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 212 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Pajak Penghasilan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 12), including the 2021 single-transaction amendment (adopted as an amendment to PSAK 46, effective 1 January 2023, per the independent recheck). STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","attrs":{"textAlign":null},"content":[{"text":"PSAK 208 ","type":"text"},{"text":"Kebijakan Akuntansi, Perubahan Estimasi Akuntansi, dan Kesalahan","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (IAS 8), hierarchy and consistency. STANDARD — NOT VERIFIED.","type":"text"}]}]}]}]}$j$::jsonb,
       presentation = $p${"deck":"When money in a bank account stops being cash, where the twelve-month line falls, and why the abandonment fund never nets against the obligation it exists to pay","genre":null,"author_bio":null,"references":[],"hero_caption":null,"key_takeaways":["A bank balance leaves cash and cash equivalents when the entity cannot draw it on demand. An Indonesian contractor's abandonment fund sits in a joint account with SKK Migas, and a debt service reserve sits in an account the lenders control, so both are restricted cash.","The current/non-current split follows when the restriction lifts or the money is spent. The same deposit can change sides without moving, and no such reclassification is a cash flow.","The abandonment fund and the abandonment provision are presented gross. No standard lets one cancel the other, and under a cost-recovery contract any surplus left in the fund at the end belongs to the state, which caps what the fund is worth to the contractor."],"revision_note":null}$p$::jsonb,
       updated_at = now()
 WHERE id = '6958ca4f-381d-4d4e-af64-81f53169a422';
